---
title: Add a sound effect
description: Add a one-shot or sequenced sound effect to your mod and play it with a SOUND constant.
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

A sound effect is a file in `audio/sfx/`, and the build names its `SOUND_` constant after the file, so `shop_bell.xml` becomes `SOUND_SHOP_BELL`. A sound either plays one sample (a one-shot) or runs a short script that plays several notes (a sequence). In this guide, you add one of each using samples that the game already has, then play them from code. To use your own recording, first [add an instrument to a sound bank](/papermario-dx/audio/instruments).

## Add a one-shot

1. Create `audio/sfx/shop_bell.xml`.

    The file name must start with a lowercase letter and contain only lowercase letters, digits, and underscores.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SoundEffect>
	<Routing allocation="dynamic" maxPlayer="7" priority="0"/>
	<OneShot wav="open_popup" volume="70" lockVolume="true" lockPitch="true"/>
</SoundEffect>
```

2. Rebuild. The build gives the sound the constant `SOUND_SHOP_BELL`, which you use as described in [Play the sound](#play-the-sound).

`OneShot` uses these attributes:

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `wav` | The sample to play. It's the name of an instrument in an always-loaded bank, without `.wav`. |
| `volume` | The volume in hexadecimal, from `00` to `FF`. |
| `lockVolume`, `lockPitch` | Keep your values when game code plays the sound with its own volume or pitch. `lockPan` and `lockReverb` work the same way. |
| `pan`, `reverb`, `pitch`, `randomPitch`, `envelope` | Optional. If you leave them out, the defaults apply. |

`Routing` decides which of the eight sound effect players can play the sound and how it competes with sounds that are already playing:

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `allocation="dynamic"` | The engine picks a player when the sound starts. Use `allocation="fixed"` with a `player` value to always use one player. |
| `maxPlayer` | The highest player number the engine can pick, from `0` to `7`. |
| `priority` | From `0` (lowest) to `3` (highest). A sound can replace a playing sound of equal or lower priority. |

If you copy the `Routing` line from a similar sound in the game's `audio/sfx/`, the new sound behaves the same way.

## Add a sequence

Use a sequence when the sound needs several notes, timing, loops, or more than one track. A sequence has `Tracks` instead of a `OneShot`, and it needs a `Routing` element.

1. Create `audio/sfx/shop_jingle.xml`.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SoundEffect>
	<Routing allocation="dynamic" maxPlayer="7" priority="0"/>
	<Tracks>
		<Track slot="0">
			<Sequence lockVolume="true" lockPitch="true">
				<SetVolume volume="69"/>
				<SetReverb reverb="5"/>
				<SetInstrument wav="menu_show_choice"/>
				<Play pitch="60" velocity="110" length="4"/>
				<Delay ticks="12"/>
				<Play pitch="64" velocity="110" length="4"/>
				<Delay ticks="12"/>
				<Play pitch="67" velocity="110" length="19"/>
				<Delay ticks="48"/>
				<End/>
			</Sequence>
		</Track>
	</Tracks>
</SoundEffect>
```

2. Rebuild. The build gives the sound the constant `SOUND_SHOP_JINGLE`.

`Play` starts a note. `pitch` is a note number, `velocity` runs from `0` to `127`, and `length` is in ticks. `Delay` waits for the given number of ticks before the next command. `End` finishes the sequence. The game's sequences in `audio/sfx/` show the other commands, such as `StartLoop`, `EndLoop`, and `PitchSweep`. Copy one that behaves like the sound you want, such as the game's `audio/sfx/menu_show_choice.xml`, and change it.

A sequence can pick a press envelope with `<SetPressEnvelope ref="envelope03"/>`. Each envelope is a file in `audio/sfx/envelopes/`, named after the envelope, so you add one by creating `audio/sfx/envelopes/<name>.xml`.

## Play the sound

Use the constant anywhere you play a sound.

In C:

```c
sfx_play_sound(SOUND_SHOP_BELL);
```

In an EVT script:

```c
Call(PlaySound, SOUND_SHOP_BELL)
```

Rebuild, then run the code in the game to hear the sound. The Audio Booth in [Star Rod](/papermario-dx/star-rod) also previews a sound effect from its source files.

## Replace or remove a game sound

To replace a sound, save your version with the same file name in your layer, such as `audio/sfx/chime_begin_ambush.xml`. It keeps the sound's constant, so existing code plays your version.

To remove a sound, create `audio/sfx/chime_begin_ambush.xml.meta` containing `delete: true`. The constant `SOUND_CHIME_BEGIN_AMBUSH` then no longer exists, so remove or change the code that uses it first.

A `SoundEffect` with no children is silent. The game's `audio/sfx/hit_silent.xml` is one.

